# How to cite the registry. GitHub shows it as "Cite this repository", and Zenodo reads it
# when it archives a GitHub release. The version and release date are not kept here:
# Zenodo takes them from each release, and gives each release its own DOI. `doi` is
# the concept DOI, which names every version.
# Zenodo takes one licence only: a list makes it fail with "Citation metadata load
# failed". So `license` names the code's licence, and the abstract names the data's.
cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  If you use the Open Interpretability registry or the openinterp package, please cite
  it as below. To name an exact version, cite the Zenodo DOI of that release.
title: "Open Interpretability: an agent-audited registry of native neurons in open-weight models"
type: software
doi: 10.5281/zenodo.24150022
authors:
  - given-names: Will
    family-names: Sackfield
repository-code: "https://github.com/8W9aG/openinterpretability"
url: "https://open-interpretability.com/"
abstract: >-
  openinterp is a decentralized, agent-audited mechanistic interpretability registry and
  testing protocol for open-weight foundation models. Each entry is one pole of one MLP
  neuron (or one attention head or dictionary latent). A proposer agent writes a
  falsifiable hypothesis from the unit's top-activating examples, a reviewer agent writes
  adversarial probes, and the verdict comes from measuring those probes on the model, so
  anyone with the weights can reproduce it. The code is under the MIT licence. The data
  (the records and pages under wiki/) is under CC-BY-4.0.
keywords:
  - mechanistic interpretability
  - neurons
  - large language models
  - explanation scoring
  - reproducibility
license: MIT
